@* What a page ranks against: everything you collect, one chase list, or one set.

   Four pages ask this question — Packs, Trades, the trade board and Wonder Pick — and each had
   written the control out longhand. The chase list half was byte-identical in all four, so a chase list
   renamed or a list of one behaving differently from a list of many had to be fixed four times, and
   twice it had been fixed in only some of them.

   What is left as parameters is what the pages genuinely differ on, not what they happened to
   type differently. The value this produces is read back by AppSession.TargetForScope, which
   accepts every spelling any of these pages ever emitted. *@

@inject AppSession Session

<select class="form-select form-select-sm" style="max-width:16rem"
        aria-label="@Label" @onchange="OnScope">
    <option value="everything" selected="@(Scope == "everything")">@Everything</option>

    @* Chase lists sit in the same control as sets because they are the same thing to the engine:
       a target. A separate "chase list mode" would imply the ranking works differently for them. *@
    @if (Session.ChaseLists.Count > 0)
    {
        <optgroup label="Chase Lists">
            @* Every list at once, merged. Demands overlapping between lists count once, so two
               lists that both want the same card do not double its price. Pointless with one
               list, which is why it is not simply always offered. *@
            @if (Merged && Session.ChaseLists.Count > 1)
            {
                <option value="chases" selected="@(Scope == "chases")">
                    All chase lists together
                </option>
            }
            @foreach (var list in Session.ChaseLists)
            {
                <option value="chase:@list.Id" selected="@(Scope == $"chase:{list.Id}")">
                    @list.Name
                </option>
            }
        </optgroup>
    }

    @if (Grouped)
    {
        @foreach (var series in Session.Sets.Series)
        {
            <optgroup label="Series @series">
                @* A whole series is a scope people actually think in — "am I done with the A
                   sets" — and it sits above its own sets rather than in a separate list, so the
                   dropdown reads as one hierarchy. *@
                @if (Merged)
                {
                    <option value="series:@series" selected="@(Scope == $"series:{series}")">
                        Series @series
                    </option>
                }
                @foreach (var set in Session.SetsInSeries(series))
                {
                    <option value="@set" selected="@(Scope == set)">
                        @set &mdash; @Session.Sets.DisplayName(set)
                    </option>
                }
            </optgroup>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <optgroup label="Sets">
            @foreach (var set in Session.Index.OpenableSets.OrderBy(Session.Sets.SortKey, StringComparer.Ordinal))
            {
                <option value="set:@set" selected="@(Scope == $"set:{set}")">@set</option>
            }
        </optgroup>
    }
</select>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The accessible name. Every page words this as the question it is really asking — "what to
    /// trade for", "what counts as wanted" — because "scope" says nothing on its own.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Label { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>The first option's wording, which reads differently depending on the page.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Everything { get; set; } = "Everything I collect";

    /// <summary>The scope currently chosen, in the grammar AppSession.TargetForScope reads.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Scope { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter] public EventCallback<ChangeEventArgs> OnScope { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Sets grouped under their series rather than listed flat. Worth the extra depth only where
    /// the list is long enough to need it.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Grouped { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Also offer the combined scopes: every chase list at once, and a whole series. Only the Packs
    /// page can act on them — the others rank one target at a time.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Merged { get; set; }
}
